---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-c`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin C (Ascorbic Acid) Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: { '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Scurvy', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E54', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Vitamin C', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin C (Ascorbic Acid): Immunity, Collagen, and Actual Evidence"
  description="Complete vitamin C reference — RDA, food sources, scurvy prevention, cold-and-flu evidence, mega-dose questions, and clinical uses."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/vitamin-c", label: "Vitamin C" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin C (Ascorbic Acid)</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      A water-soluble vitamin that humans lost the ability to synthesise millions of years ago
      (via a broken GULO gene). Required for collagen hydroxylation, catecholamine synthesis,
      and as a general antioxidant. Its deficiency disease — scurvy — is rare in modern diets
      but its role in cold-and-flu prevention and mega-dosing continues to fuel research.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adult men:</strong> 90 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Adult women:</strong> 75 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Smokers:</strong> add 35 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy / lactation:</strong> 85 / 120 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Tolerable Upper Intake Level:</strong> 2,000 mg/day (above this: GI upset, kidney stone risk in susceptible people).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deficiency and scurvy</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      True scurvy requires vitamin C intake below ~10 mg/day for weeks to months. Early signs:
      fatigue, easy bruising, corkscrew hairs. Late signs: gum bleeding and tooth loss,
      petechiae, poor wound healing, joint pain, and if untreated, death from cardiac
      failure or haemorrhage. Modern cases occur in alcoholism, extreme "meat-only" diets,
      severe eating disorders, and institutional settings.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cold and flu — what the evidence shows</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      The Hemilä Cochrane review (updated 2013, ~11,000 participants) shows regular vitamin C
      supplementation (200 mg+/day) does <strong>not</strong> reduce common-cold incidence in
      the general population. It does <strong>modestly reduce cold duration</strong> — about
      8% in adults and 14% in children.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      In extreme physical stress (marathon runners, soldiers on Arctic training), regular
      supplementation halves cold incidence. Once a cold has started, therapeutic
      supplementation shows no clear benefit — the effect is preventive, not curative.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">High-dose IV vitamin C in cancer and sepsis</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Oral vitamin C absorption saturates at ~400 mg per dose. Intravenous administration
      achieves much higher plasma concentrations. Randomised trials of IV vitamin C in
      advanced cancer (mostly single-arm or non-randomised) suggest quality-of-life
      improvement but not survival benefit. In septic shock, the CITRIS-ALI trial and
      VITAMINS trial were negative for mortality reduction. IV vitamin C remains
      investigational.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Red bell pepper (½ cup raw): 95 mg</li>
      <li>Orange (1 medium): 70 mg</li>
      <li>Kiwifruit (1 medium): 64 mg</li>
      <li>Broccoli (½ cup cooked): 51 mg</li>
      <li>Strawberries (½ cup): 49 mg</li>
      <li>Brussels sprouts, potatoes, tomatoes, spinach — 15–50 mg per serving</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      Vitamin C is easily destroyed by heat, light, and prolonged cooking. Raw or lightly
      cooked vegetables and fruit preserve the most.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Supplementation</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Ascorbic acid</strong> — standard, cheapest, most studied.</li>
      <li><strong>Buffered forms</strong> (calcium/sodium ascorbate) — gentler on the stomach.</li>
      <li><strong>Liposomal vitamin C</strong> — claims of higher bioavailability; some evidence, higher cost.</li>
      <li>Typical maintenance: 100–500 mg/day. High doses (&gt; 1,000 mg/day) offer no clear extra benefit and can cause diarrhoea and kidney stones in predisposed individuals.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Consult your physician before high-dose vitamin C
      if you have a history of kidney stones, iron overload disorders (haemochromatosis),
      or glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-c" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
